---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import { MorseRunnerGame } from '../../components/Games/games'

const title = 'Morse Runner — A Morse Code Sending Game | MorseForge'
const description =
  'Gates come towards you, each asking for one character. Key it with the straight key before you arrive. A Morse code game about sending under time pressure.'
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path="/games/morse-runner/">
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-14">
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">Morse Runner</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted">
      Gates come towards you. Key the character before you get there.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-10">
      <MorseRunnerGame client:load />
    </div>

    <section class="mt-16 border-t border-line pt-10">
      <h2 class="font-display text-xl font-bold tracking-tight">Why sending needs a clock</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted">
        The <a href="/telegraph/" class="text-signal underline underline-offset-4">telegraph</a> waits
        for you forever, and so does the sending drill. That is the right way to learn a character — and
        it is why neither of them ever teaches you to commit to one. Hesitating costs nothing there. On
        the air it costs the contact.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Here the gate arrives whether you are ready or not, which is the only honest way to find out
        whether you can send a character or merely work one out.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-8 font-display text-xl font-bold tracking-tight">If gates keep landing</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted">
        How long a pause ends your character is a separate setting from speed, and the default is
        deliberately patient. If characters are committing before you have finished them — or not
        committing at all — adjust the keyer in
        <a href="/settings/" class="text-signal underline underline-offset-4">settings</a> rather than
        fighting it.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-8 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        The other one is <a
          href="/games/signal-defender/"
          class="text-signal underline underline-offset-4">Signal Defender</a
        >, which is the same idea for listening.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
